  "details": "Now, I'm all for it when it comes to optimization and improving performance. I make a lot of avatars and have an off-sorts off-radar kinda-sorta-but-not-really private collection of avatars I make for public use.\nHowever with the latest update to the dynamic bones, I feel a bit kicked in the teeth here and a lack of drive to really optimize models if my target audience (New users / public worlds) can't even see the fancy smancy your hair is realistically drapey and doesn't kill my PC because of the usual absolutely grotesque count of dynamic bones and collider work I put into them. Yes, they can manually change/disable these settings on their own. But someone with less than a few hours into the game probably won't know or even consider going through the trouble to do so.\n8 Collision Checks is very difficult to work with, and so far I have only been able to convert one out of the 20+ models I have spent countless long hours slowly working on and turning them into a high-performance, high-quality dream as they're called.\n\nNow I'm not going crazy here I don't think when saying that 16 is incredibly better than 8, and I have never really noticed a difference to performance when it comes to Dynamic Bones. Heck, I'd be happy even with 12.\nI run VR with an I5 and a GTX 1050, so performance to me is a very important thing and fully understand that there are people out there whose computers are for better or worse.",
